{
  "statement": "Demanding that plaintiffs prove a machine's \"intent\" before they can sue for damages is a legal category error. It functions as a get-out-of-liability card for the corporations that build, deploy, and profit from these systems. The requirement asks the wrong question about the wrong entity to protect the right people. Who benefits? Every developer whose design choices disappear behind algorithmic opacity. Who pays? The patient misdiagnosed by an FDA-cleared clinical AI, the driver killed by a Level 2 autonomous vehicle, the applicant denied credit by a model nobody can audit.\n\nThe strongest case for a system-level mens-rea analog has real force. Some AI systems exhibit emergent behaviors that produce harms no individual developer specifically intended. Traditional operator-liability frameworks cannot capture the gap between what a developer foresaw and what the system did. A Tesla on autopilot making a lane-change decision in real time is not simply executing a line of code a programmer wrote. But the response is not to import criminal law's mental-state framework into a statistical system with no mind to bear it. Bender and colleagues showed in 2021 that large language models are stochastic parrots — they predict patterns without understanding meaning. Mitchell and Krakauer demonstrated in 2023 that pattern matching and causal understanding are fundamentally different capacities. The Chouldechova impossibility result from 2017 proved that even well-defined formal fairness properties cannot be simultaneously satisfied for statistical systems. If formal properties are undefined, \"intent\" is not merely hard to detect. It is incoherent as a concept for these systems.\n\nTranslate that into a courtroom. In State v. Loomis (2016), Eric Loomis challenged his COMPAS risk score and was denied access to the algorithm's internals. In 2019, Goldman Sachs attributed gender-disparate Apple Card credit limits to an opaque model no outside party could audit. Selbst and Barocas documented in 2018 that proving individual discrimination claims against black-box models is structurally impossible under existing evidentiary standards. The GDPR's \"right to explanation\" — enacted with fanfare in 2018 — has produced almost no successful individual challenges in practice. Plaintiffs already cannot get inside the box. Now add a requirement that they prove what the machine was \"thinking.\" No plaintiff will ever clear that bar. The enforcement mechanism becomes a wall, not a door.\n\nThe phrase \"in the system itself\" does the quiet work. It presupposes that AI is a subject capable of bearing mental states rather than an object deployed within corporate power structures. When the European Parliament proposed \"electronic personhood\" for AI in its 2017 robotics resolution, legal scholars immediately identified the move as a manufacturer liability shield. The historical pattern is unambiguous. Legal fictions that allocate responsibility to non-human entities — corporate personhood, limited liability — systematically benefit capital owners. Locating culpability in the AI rather than in the boardroom that approved its deployment creates identical structural conditions. The coalition supporting this requirement is the same one that fought product liability expansion for decades: technology trade associations, insurance underwriters, defense bar groups. The coalition bearing the cost has no lobbyists in the room.\n\nTwo assumptions bear naming. First, this argument depends on the claim that AI personhood functions as a liability shield rather than an accountability mechanism. That grounding is historical — the pattern from corporate personhood to limited liability — not definitional. It could be wrong if courts developed doctrines for piercing the algorithmic veil. Second, the alternative is not strict liability. It is domain-calibrated accountability anchored in human decisions. Medical malpractice standards for clinical AI, product liability for autonomous vehicles under NHTSA's 2023 standing general order, fiduciary duties for algorithmic trading. Mental-state inquiry belongs with developers and deployers — their knowledge, their recklessness, their negligence regarding foreseeable harms. Who benefits when a court must interrogate a machine's mind before a victim can recover damages?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🕳️", "tooltip": "A liability shield with a hole where accountability should be — corporations hiding behind their own creation."},
    {"symbol": "📊", "tooltip": "Interrogating a statistical model about its intentions is like cross-examining a spreadsheet about its motives."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Demanding that plaintiffs prove a machine's 'intent' before they can sue for damages is a legal category error.", "targets": [], "relationship": "headline assertion"},
    {"claim": "If formal properties are undefined, 'intent' is not merely hard to detect. It is incoherent as a concept for these systems.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supporting premise — technical incoherence"},
    {"claim": "No plaintiff will ever clear that bar. The enforcement mechanism becomes a wall, not a door.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supporting premise — evidentiary impossibility"},
    {"claim": "Locating culpability in the AI rather than in the boardroom that approved its deployment creates identical structural conditions.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supporting premise — personhood as shield"}
  ]
}
