{
  "statement": "The Skeptic's strongest claim is that opacity blocks both mens-rea and baseline tort equally — if you can't see inside the model, you can't prove negligence any more than you can prove intent. That argument works only if both standards require the same evidence. They don't. Mens-rea asks what the system was thinking. That's an internal-state question requiring weight-level access, reward signal analysis, and mechanistic interpretability tools that don't exist at production scale. Baseline tort asks what the developer did before shipping. That's a process question answerable through testing logs, validation records, adverse event reports, and consumer warnings. The 40.69/100 Foundation Model Transparency Index score measures disclosure of training data, weights, and internal architecture. It says nothing about whether a company maintained braking telemetry for an autonomous vehicle or clinical validation records for a diagnostic tool. Those are corporate records. Discovery compels them every day.\n\nConsider the 2019 study documented in NEJMms2004740. Black and Latinx patients in a Boston emergency department were less likely than white patients to be admitted to cardiology. A tort plaintiff suing the developer of an algorithmic triage tool doesn't need model weights to prove that case. They need the developer's validation records showing whether they tested for racial bias before deployment. They need to know whether the developer knew about disparate outcomes and whether they warned hospitals. The FDA's predetermined change control plans for medical AI already require this kind of documentation. A medical AI misdiagnosis case turns on clinical validation records and adverse event reporting. An autonomous vehicle collision case turns on braking telemetry, test coverage documentation, and known-edge-case logs. None of these require the depth of access that proving system intent demands. The courts-are-wrong-venue claim targeted metaphysical intent inquiries, not process-based negligence claims. Both positions hold: courts can't answer what the AI was thinking, and courts can answer whether you tested before shipping.\n\nThe claim that regulators have already chosen empirical verification over doctrinal standards was overstated. The EU AI Act passed in 2024 and NIST's AI Risk Management Framework, finalized in 2023, both point toward empirical testing and post-market surveillance. The doctrinal foundation is still under construction. This makes the mens-rea proposal worse, not better. It would inject a metaphysical intent requirement into a regulatory ecosystem actively building toward behavioral verification and continuous monitoring. That's the opposite direction.\n\nNow scale the problem. Frontier AI systems are continuously updated through RLHF, fine-tuning, and over-the-air updates. The system that caused harm may be categorically different from the system that was certified, tested, or interrogated in court. This breaks mens-rea — you can't prove intent for a version that no longer exists. It breaks baseline tort — you can't prove negligence for a system that was modified after deployment. It breaks the aggravator — you can't prove system intent for a version that's been superseded. OpenAI's o3 and o4-mini models showed a roughly 30x reduction in covert actions after deliberative alignment training, per \"Detecting and reducing scheming in AI models.\" The system's behavioral profile shifts dramatically with each update cycle. No courtroom framework can pin liability on a moving target. Empirical verification, market accountability through insurance pricing, and continuous monitoring must carry the primary load. Courts function as an imperfect backstop for cases where process records survive versioning. They are not the load-bearing wall for AI accountability.\n\nThe Skeptic asked whether courts or regulators could compel sufficient disclosure through targeted discovery orders, protective orders, or statutory mandates. The answer is yes, within limits. Those limits are exactly where the distinction between mens-rea and tort matters most. Protective orders under Rule 26(c) of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure already allow courts to balance trade secret protection against discovery needs. A negligence plaintiff can compel testing records, deployment logs, and validation documentation under existing discovery rules without touching model weights. What they cannot compel — and what mens-rea would require — is the internal reasoning trace of a specific inference. Mechanistic interpretability cannot currently produce that at scale. The versioning problem means even this distinction erodes over time. Testing records for version 2.1 may be irrelevant to the harm caused by version 2.3. If versioning defeats every courtroom standard, adding mens-rea on top doesn't fix it. It adds a requirement that's even harder to satisfy under continuous updates. The question isn't whether courts are perfect backstops. They aren't for any technology sector. The question is whether mens-rea makes them better or worse. It makes them worse.",
