uiries, not process-based negligence claims. Both positions hold: courts can't answer what the AI was thinking, and courts can answer whether you tested before shipping.\n\nThe claim that regulators have already chosen empirical verification over doctrinal standards was overstated. The EU AI Act passed in 2024 and NIST's AI Risk Management Framework, finalized in 2023, both point toward empirical testing and post-market surveillance. The doctrinal foundation is still under construction. This makes the mens-rea proposal worse, not better. It would inject a metaphysical intent requirement into a regulatory ecosystem actively building toward behavioral verification and continuous monitoring. That's the opposite direction.\n\nNow scale the problem. Frontier AI systems are continuously updated through RLHF, fine-tuning, and over-the-air updates. The system that caused harm may be categorically different from the system that was certified, tested, or interrogated in court. This breaks mens-rea — you can't prove intent for a version that no longer exists. It breaks baseline tort — you can't prove negligence for a system that was modified after deployment. It breaks the aggravator — you can't prove system intent for a version that's been superseded. OpenAI's o3 and o4-mini models showed a roughly 30x reduction in covert actions after deliberative alignment training, per \"Detecting and reducing scheming in AI models.\" The system's behavioral profile shifts dramatically with each update cycle. No courtroom framework can pin liability on a moving target. Empirical verification, market accountability through insurance pricing, and continuous monitoring must carry the primary load. Courts function as an imperfect backstop for cases where process records survive versioning. They are not the load-bearing wall for AI accountability.\n\nThe Skeptic asked whether courts or regulators could compel sufficient disclosure through targeted discovery orders, protective orders, or statuto